# Copy to cuttingfloor.env (git-ignored) and fill in; up.sh copies it to the server, mode 600.
CUTTINGFLOOR_DOMAIN=mcp.example.com
CUTTINGFLOOR_PUBLIC_URL=https://mcp.example.com
# OAuth: the authorization server clients sign in through (WorkOS AuthKit domain). Register
# CUTTINGFLOOR_PUBLIC_URL/mcp there as the default resource indicator, and turn on CIMD and DCR.
CUTTINGFLOOR_OAUTH_ISSUER=https://your-env.authkit.app
# WorkOS API key (production environment), for account checks. With OAuth on, the server won't
# start without it and STRIPE_SECRET_KEY: without them every sign-in would get in. The credit
# ledger lives in the record database (CUTTINGFLOOR_RECORDS=mongo below).
WORKOS_API_KEY=
# Stripe: packs bought, refunds, saved cards and automatic top-up.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Kazi's partner API, for the hand-off into Kazi after a sign-up on our site (optional): its base
# URL and the bearer key this server calls it with (Kazi holds only its SHA-256). Kazi keeps no
# cuttingfloor credits.
KAZI_CREDITS_URL=https://app.apifunnel.ai
KAZI_CREDITS_KEY=
# Stripe's webhook signing secret for <CUTTINGFLOOR_PUBLIC_URL>/stripe/webhook, which credits
# cuttingfloor's packs and takes back refunds (events payment_intent.succeeded, charge.refunded,
# refund.created, refund.updated). Made by scripts/stripe-setup.ts.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# App Store Sandbox purchases (TestFlight, App Review, a tester's sandbox account) cost nothing,
# so they add credits only for staff and these accounts' emails, comma-separated. Anyone else's is
# acknowledged and adds nothing.
CUTTINGFLOOR_APPLE_SANDBOX_EMAILS=
# Where people subscribe and manage billing; named in refusals and email.
CUTTINGFLOOR_ACCOUNT_URL=https://cuttingfloor.ai/account
# Lets the site read an account's credits (GET /account/usage); the same value is the
# site's CUTTINGFLOOR_ACCOUNT_KEY in Vercel.
CUTTINGFLOOR_ACCOUNT_KEY=
# Encrypts the provider keys users save on the account page (stored as ciphertext in their WorkOS
# metadata). 32 bytes, base64: openssl rand -base64 32. Changing it makes saved keys unreadable.
CUTTINGFLOOR_KEY_SECRET=
# Signs download, watch and upload links: openssl rand -base64 32, the same on every server. Unset, each server keeps its own key in secret.key under its data root.
CUTTINGFLOOR_URL_SECRET=
# Where records are kept (timelines, media and job records, keys, meter, notices): fs, the JSON
# files under the data root (the default), or mongo, a MongoDB database every server shares.
# Files (media, uploads, exports) stay under the data root either way.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_RECORDS=mongo
# The database, one of two ways. As its parts: the host, and a database user and password (any
# characters; they are escaped for you, and a password needs its user). A host ending in
# .mongodb.net with no port is an Atlas cluster, looked up as SRV; any other host (mongo,
# 10.0.0.5:27017, several with commas) is plain mongodb://. Start it with mongodb:// or
# mongodb+srv:// to say which yourself.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_MONGODB_HOST=cuttingfloor.a662a.mongodb.net
# CUTTINGFLOOR_MONGODB_USER=
# CUTTINGFLOOR_MONGODB_PASSWORD=
# Or as one connection string, which wins when set (escape the password yourself):
# CUTTINGFLOOR_MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://USER:PASSWORD@cuttingfloor.a662a.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority
# The database's name. Default cuttingfloor.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_MONGODB_DB=cuttingfloor
# Where files are kept (media, uploads, exports, analyses, derived files): fs, under the data
# root (the default), or s3, a bucket every server shares. With s3 the data root holds only this
# server's caches. A missing or wrong setting below stops the server at start, naming it.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_BLOBS=s3
# The bucket: its S3 endpoint, region and name, and a key that may read and write it. The bucket
# is addressed as a host under the endpoint (https://BUCKET.fsn1.your-objectstorage.com), and
# pages that play media are allowed that origin. The keys are never logged or returned.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_ENDPOINT=https://fsn1.your-objectstorage.com
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_REGION=fsn1
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_BUCKET=cuttingfloor-prod
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# 1 addresses the bucket as a path on the endpoint instead (MinIO).
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_PATH_STYLE=
# This server's copies of the files engines read: a folder on its fastest disk (default
# cache/blobs under the data root) and how many GB it may hold before the least recently used
# go (default 50). Nothing in it is the only copy.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_BLOB_CACHE=/data/cache/blobs
# CUTTINGFLOOR_BLOB_CACHE_GB=50
# Transfers: the size of a part in MB (default 16), parts of one file sent or fetched at once
# (default 6), and requests to the bucket at once from this server, all together (default 64;
# the bucket allows 256 connections per address).
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_PART_MB=16
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_CONCURRENCY=6
# CUTTINGFLOOR_S3_CONNECTIONS=64
# A second server on a copy of production's data, until the cutover: 1 makes it answer requests
# and do nothing by the clock (no sweeps, no job recovery, no account emails or sequence, no
# email at all). Unset it and restart at the cutover. See README, "Standby".
# CUTTINGFLOOR_STANDBY=1
# Partners that get a script key for each of their users (POST /partner/keys {email, sub}, with the
# partner's secret as the bearer), as <name>=<sha256 of the secret>, comma-separated. Only the hash
# is kept here.
# Make one: s=cfp_$(openssl rand -hex 32); printf %s "$s" | shasum -a 256
# CUTTINGFLOOR_PARTNER_KEYS=Kazi=
# The token OpenAI's plugin portal shows for domain verification; served as plain text at
# /.well-known/openai-apps-challenge. Unset serves nothing there.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_OPENAI_CHALLENGE=
# Resend, for account email: a welcome while the sign-up sequence is off, 80% and 100% of
# credits, a failed payment; and the sign-up sequence below. From support@cuttingfloor.ai, or from
# CUTTINGFLOOR_EMAIL_FROM (replies to CUTTINGFLOOR_EMAIL_REPLY_TO) when set.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# The sign-up sequence (apps/hosted/src/sequence.ts): a welcome within a minute of signing up, then
# one follow-up. Off until all four are set. FROM is a person's name on a verified Resend domain,
# never a noreply address; REPLY_TO is an inbox someone reads, because the emails ask for replies.
CUTTINGFLOOR_EMAIL_FROM="Your Name at cuttingfloor <you@cuttingfloor.ai>"
CUTTINGFLOOR_EMAIL_REPLY_TO=support@cuttingfloor.ai
# Who signs the emails, e.g. a first name.
CUTTINGFLOOR_EMAIL_SIGNER=
# Signs the one-click unsubscribe links: openssl rand -base64 32. Changing it breaks links already sent.
CUTTINGFLOOR_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=
# Resend's signing secret (whsec_…) for the webhook at <CUTTINGFLOOR_PUBLIC_URL>/email/events, with
# the email.bounced and email.complained events: those addresses get nothing more.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# When the follow-up goes, in hours after signing up, and how often new accounts are looked for.
# CUTTINGFLOOR_FOLLOW_UP_AFTER_HOURS=72
# CUTTINGFLOOR_CREDITS_AFTER_HOURS=168
# CUTTINGFLOOR_SEQUENCE_POLL_SECONDS=30
# The email copy, read at every send. compose.yaml mounts /opt/cuttingfloor/email here, which
# up.sh copies from apps/hosted/email; unset uses the copy inside the image.
CUTTINGFLOOR_EMAIL_TEMPLATES=/etc/cuttingfloor/email
# owner:key pairs, comma-separated; each key is a client's bearer token, its owner the account.
CUTTINGFLOOR_API_KEYS=ken:cf_change_me
# The server's own TypeSafe key: answers judge.ask and media.analyze look for every account.
TYPESAFE_API_KEY=
# Jev judgments each account may ask a day when its plan doesn't say (API keys; hosted default
# 5000): one a look frame; one a judge.ask call, plus one per picture past the first. Signed-in
# accounts get their plan's (packages/plans). CUTTINGFLOOR_LOOK_FRAMES_PER_DAY still works.
CUTTINGFLOOR_JUDGMENTS_PER_DAY=5000

# Local only, for scripts/smoke-account.ts on previews: Vercel automation bypass secret.
# VERCEL_BYPASS=
